### TL;DR  
The rule  

```js
if (layer.type === 'symbol' &&
    layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // flag as “route shield”
}
```  

is **not a reliable way to spot route‑shield symbols**.  
`text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when it isn’t set, and many plain place‑name layers also use `symbol-placement: "point"` with the default anchor. In other words, you’re flagging *any* point‑placed symbol that doesn’t explicitly set an anchor – which is a huge portion of typical label layers.

A more robust heuristic should look at **what makes a shield a shield**, i.e. the *combination* of an icon (the shield graphic) **and** a text field (the route number) that are deliberately allowed to overlap and ignore placement. Below are the properties that, together, are a far better indicator:

| Property | Why it matters for shields |
|----------|----------------------------|
| `layout.icon-image` | The sprite that draws the shield shape (usually a name containing “shield”, “road‑shield”, “route‑*”, etc.). |
| `layout.text-field` | The placeholder that injects the route reference (`{ref}`, `{ref:short}`, `{shield_text}`, etc.). |
| `layout.icon-allow-overlap` **or** `layout.icon-ignore-placement` | Shields are meant to sit on top of other symbols; they usually ignore placement rules. |
| `layout.text-allow-overlap` **or** `layout.text-ignore-placement` | Same reason for the text part of the shield. |
| `layout.icon-size` (or a data‑driven expression) | Shields often have a fixed size or a size that scales with zoom. |
| `paint['icon-color']` / `paint['text-color']` | Shields often use a contrasting color (white text on a colored icon). |
| `layout.icon-pitch-alignment` / `layout.text-pitch-alignment` set to `"viewport"` | Keeps the shield upright regardless of map pitch. |
| `layout.icon-optional` (optional) | Some styles make the icon optional when the sprite is missing – a pattern you’ll see in shield layers. |

If you combine **at least two** of the above (most commonly `icon-image` **and** `text-field`) you’ll capture the vast majority of true shield layers while ignoring ordinary place‑name labels.

---

## 1. Why the original heuristic fails

| Situation | `text-anchor` | `symbol-placement` | Result of your test |
|-----------|---------------|--------------------|---------------------|
| **Place‑name point label** (e.g. city name) | *undefined* (defaults to `"center"`) | `"point"` | **Flagged** (false positive) |
| **Road‑shield** | *undefined* (defaults to `"center"`) | `"point"` | **Flagged** (true positive) |
| **Line‑placed label** (e.g. road name) | *any* | `"line"` | Not flagged (good) |
| **Icon‑only point symbol** (e.g. POI) | *undefined* | `"point"` | **Flagged** (false positive) |

Because `text-anchor` is *optional* in the spec, its absence tells you nothing about the intent of the layer. The only thing you’re really testing is “is this a point‑placed symbol that doesn’t override the default anchor?” – a condition that is true for **most** point symbols.

---

## 2. A more reliable heuristic

Below is a **minimal** yet effective rule‑set you can drop into your audit script. Feel free to tighten or loosen it based on the specifics of the style you’re checking.

```js
function isPotentialShield(layer) {
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣ Must have BOTH an icon and a text field.
  const hasIcon   = typeof layout['icon-image'] === 'string' && layout['icon-image'].length > 0;
  const hasText   = typeof layout['text-field']   === 'string' && layout['text-field'].length > 0;
  if (!hasIcon || !hasText) return false;

  // 2️⃣ The icon name usually contains a hint that it’s a shield.
  const iconName = layout['icon-image'];
  const shieldKeywords = ['shield', 'road', 'route', 'sign', 'marker'];
  const looksLikeShield = shieldKeywords.some(k => iconName.toLowerCase().includes(k));
  if (!looksLikeShield) {
    // Some styles use generic names like "road-1", "road-2", etc.
    // If you have a sprite sheet you can also check the sprite dimensions.
    // For now we’ll be permissive and just continue.
  }

  // 3️⃣ Overlap/ignore‑placement flags are almost always on for shields.
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true ||
                       layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['text-ignore-placement'] === true;

  // 4️⃣ Optional: make sure the icon is anchored to the viewport (keeps it upright).
  const viewportAligned = layout['icon-pitch-alignment'] === 'viewport' ||
                          layout['text-pitch-alignment'] === 'viewport';

  // If we have the core combo (icon + text) and at least one of the
  // “shield‑like” flags, treat it as a shield.
  return (hasIcon && hasText) && (allowOverlap || viewportAligned);
}
```

### What this does

| Step | Reason |
|------|--------|
| **Icon + Text** | Shields are *always* a graphic **plus** a label (the route number). Plain place‑name layers usually have **only** `text-field`. |
| **Keyword check** | Many styles name their shield sprites with a hint (`*_shield`, `road-*-icon`, etc.). This catches the majority of well‑named sprites while still allowing you to fall back to the other checks if the naming is opaque. |
| **Overlap / ignore‑placement** | Shields are deliberately drawn on top of everything else; they almost always set one of these flags to `true`. |
| **Viewport alignment** | Keeps the shield upright when the map is pitched – a common shield‑specific setting. |

You can tighten the rule by requiring **both** `icon-allow-overlap` **and** `text-allow-overlap`, or by demanding that the `icon-image` name matches a regular expression you define for your project.

---

## 3. Real‑world examples

| Layer name (MapLibre demo) | `icon-image` | `text-field` | Overlap flags | Verdict |
|----------------------------|--------------|--------------|---------------|---------|
| `road-shield-primary`      | `"road-shield-primary"` | `"{ref}"` | `icon-allow-overlap: true`, `text-allow-overlap: true` | **Shield** |
| `road-shield-secondary`    | `"road-shield-secondary"` | `"{ref}"` | same as above | **Shield** |
| `place-label-city`         | *none* | `"{name}"` | `text-allow-overlap: false` | **Not a shield** |
| `poi-label`                | `"poi-icon"` | `"{name}"` | `icon-allow-overlap: true` (but no route‑ref) | **Not a shield** |
| `road-label` (line‑placed) | *none* | `"{name}"` | `symbol-placement: "line"` | **Not a shield** |

Notice how the **only** layers that satisfy the *icon + text* combo with overlap flags are the true shields.

---

## 4. Edge cases & how to handle them

| Edge case | Why it trips the heuristic | Suggested tweak |
|-----------|----------------------------|-----------------|
| **Shield sprites named generically** (e.g. `"road-1"`, `"road-2"`) | Keyword check fails. | Keep the keyword check optional – if the layer has `icon-image` **and** `text-field` **and** overlap flags, treat it as a shield even if the name doesn’t contain a keyword. |
| **Place‑name layers that also use an icon** (e.g. a city dot with a label) | They have both `icon-image` and `text-field`. | Add a second guard: the `text-field` should reference a *reference* property (`{ref}`, `{ref:short}`, `{shield_text}`) rather than a generic name field (`{name}`). If you can inspect the source data schema, look for a property that holds a route number. |
| **Dynamic icons via expressions** (`icon-image: ["case", ["==", ["get", "type"], "highway"], "shield-high", "default"]`) | The value isn’t a plain string. | Detect that `icon-image` is an expression *and* that the expression references a property that looks like a route type (`"highway"`, `"primary"`, etc.). If the expression ultimately resolves to a shield sprite, you can still flag it. |
| **Shields that are rendered as *text‑only* (e.g., using a special font instead of a sprite)** | No `icon-image`. | Look for a `text-font` that is a known shield font (e.g., `"Open Sans Bold"` with a specific `text-size` and `text-allow-overlap`). This is rarer but can be added as an extra branch. |

---

## 5. Putting it all together – a full audit snippet

```js
function auditLayers(layers) {
  const problems = [];

  layers.forEach(layer => {
    // 1️⃣ Only care about symbol layers
    if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return;

    const layout = layer.layout || {};

    // 2️⃣ Quick filter: must have a text field (most shields do)
    if (!layout['text-field']) return;

    // 3️⃣ Determine if this is a shield
    const isShield = isPotentialShield(layer);

    // 4️⃣ If the layer *looks* like a shield but is missing a key property,
    //    raise a warning (e.g., missing overlap flags)
    if (isShield) {
      const missing = [];

      if (!layout['icon-image']) missing.push('icon-image');
      if (!layout['icon-allow-overlap'] && !layout['icon-ignore-placement'])
        missing.push('icon-allow-overlap / icon-ignore-placement');
      if (!layout['text-allow-overlap'] && !layout['text-ignore-placement'])
        missing.push('text-allow-overlap / text-ignore-placement');

      if (missing.length) {
        problems.push({
          layer: layer.id,
          message: `Shield layer missing ${missing.join(', ')}`,
          severity: 'warning'
        });
      }
    } else {
      // 5️⃣ If the layer *looks* like a plain place‑name but has shield‑like flags,
      //    flag it as a possible mis‑configuration.
      const hasShieldFlags =
        layout['icon-allow-overlap'] ||
        layout['icon-ignore-placement'] ||
        layout['text-allow-overlap'] ||
        layout['text-ignore-placement'];

      if (hasShieldFlags && !layout['icon-image']) {
        problems.push({
          layer: layer.id,
          message: 'Point‑placed label with overlap flags but no icon – likely a mis‑named shield.',
          severity: 'info'
        });
      }

### TL;DR  
The rule  

```js
layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'
```  

is **not a reliable way to spot route‑shield symbols**.  
* `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` for point symbols, so most point‑placement layers will have it *undefined* even though they are ordinary place‑name labels.  
* Real shield layers are usually identified by the **presence of an icon that is a “shield” sprite** (or a sprite that is sized to the text) and by a **text field that contains a route number**.  

A better heuristic is to look at the *icon‑image* (and related properties) **and** the *text‑field* pattern, not at `text-anchor`.

---

## Why the current heuristic fails

| Property | What the spec says | What many layers actually do |
|----------|-------------------|------------------------------|
| `text-anchor` | Default = `"center"` for point placement. If you don’t set it, the property is **absent** (i.e. `undefined`). | Almost every point‑placement label (city names, POIs, etc.) leaves this undefined. |
| `symbol-placement` | `"point"` is the default for symbol layers that are not line‑or‑polygon placed. | Same default for *all* point symbols, shield or not. |

So the condition you’re using is essentially “*any point‑placement symbol layer*”, which explains why you’re flagging plain place‑name layers and missing the real shields.

---

## What a “shield” really looks like in a MapLibre style

Typical shield layers have a **combination** of the following clues:

| Clue | Example value / pattern | Why it matters |
|------|------------------------|----------------|
| **`icon-image`** | `"road-shield-1"`<br>`"us-interstate-2"`<br>`"shield-*" ` | The sprite name usually contains the word *shield*, *sign*, *road*, *route*, etc. |
| **`icon-text-fit`** | `"both"` (or `"width"`/`"height"` ) | Shields are often sized to the text they contain. |
| **`icon-text-fit-padding`** | `[2, 2, 2, 2]` | Padding around the text inside the shield sprite. |
| **`text-field`** | `"{ref}"` or `"{ref}"` where `ref` is a numeric route number | Shields display a route number, so the field is usually a numeric (or alphanumeric) token. |
| **`text-font`** | `["Open Sans Bold", "Arial Unicode MS Bold"]` | Shields often use a bold, legible font. |
| **`text-size`** | `12`–`16` (often smaller than surrounding labels) | Consistent sizing for route numbers. |
| **`text-allow-overlap` / `icon-allow-overlap`** | `true` | Shields need to sit on top of other features. |
| **`metadata`** (optional) | `{ "mapbox:group": "road-shields" }` | Some style authors add custom metadata to make identification trivial. |

> **Note:** Not every shield will have *all* of these, but a **subset** (especially `icon-image` + `icon-text-fit` + a numeric `text-field`) is a strong indicator.

---

## A more robust heuristic

Below is a **sample function** you can drop into your audit script. It tries to be tolerant of variations while still keeping false‑positives low.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route shield.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a MapLibre style layer definition
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isProbablyShield(layer) {
  // Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣ Must have an icon (the shield sprite)
  const icon = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!icon) return false;

  // 2️⃣ Icon name usually contains a shield‑related keyword
  const shieldKeywords = /shield|sign|road|route|marker|badge/i;
  if (!shieldKeywords.test(icon)) return false;

  // 3️⃣ Shields are often sized to the text they contain
  if (layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none') {
    // good sign – keep going
  } else {
    // If there is no text‑fit, we can still accept it if the icon is a known shield sprite
    // (you could maintain a whitelist of sprite names here)
  }

  // 4️⃣ Must have a text field that looks like a route number
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (!textField) return false;

  // Strip MapLibre token syntax (e.g. "{ref}" → "ref")
  const token = textField.replace(/[{}]/g, '');

  // Common route‑number patterns: 1, 1A, I‑5, US‑101, A‑7, etc.
  const routePattern = /^[A-Z]{0,2}\-?\d{1,4}[A-Z]?(?:\s?[A-Z])?$/i;
  if (!routePattern.test(token)) return false;

  // 5️⃣ Overlap settings – shields usually allow overlap
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] || layout['text-allow-overlap'];
  if (allowOverlap === false) return false; // unlikely to be a shield

  // 6️⃣ (Optional) Check for explicit metadata that style authors may have added
  if (layer.metadata && layer.metadata['mapbox:group']?.includes('shield')) {
    return true; // explicit hint – trust it
  }

  // If we made it this far, we’re pretty confident it’s a shield
  return true;
}
```

### How the function improves on the original

| Original check | New check | Why it’s better |
|----------------|-----------|-----------------|
| `text-anchor === undefined` | **Removed** – defaults to `"center"` for *all* point symbols, so it’s not discriminating. |
| `symbol-placement === 'point'` | **Kept only as a sanity filter** (implicitly true for most symbol layers). | Still ensures we’re not looking at line‑placement symbols, but it’s not the primary signal. |
| **None** | **`icon-image` presence + keyword test** | Directly looks for a sprite that *is* a shield. |
| **None** | **`icon-text-fit`** | Shields are usually sized to the text they contain. |
| **None** | **`text-field` numeric/route pattern** | Shields display a route number; place‑name labels usually show a name string. |
| **None** | **Overlap flags** | Shields need to sit on top of other features, so they often allow overlap. |
| **None** | **Metadata hint** (if the style author added it) | Gives a “golden ticket” when available. |

---

## Edge Cases & Tweaking

| Situation | How to handle it |
|-----------|------------------|
| **Shield sprite name does not contain a keyword** (e.g., `"us-101"`). | Add a **whitelist** of known sprite names for the style you’re auditing, or broaden the regex to include patterns like `^\w+-\d+`. |
| **Place‑name label that also has an icon** (e.g., a city dot). | Those usually have a *tiny* icon (often a simple circle) and **no `icon-text-fit`**. The numeric `text-field` test will also filter them out because city names are not numeric. |
| **Custom shield implementation that uses only `icon-image` (no text)**. | If you need to catch those, relax the `text-field` requirement and rely more on the sprite‑name + `icon-text-fit` + overlap flags. |
| **International styles where route numbers include non‑Latin characters**. | Extend the `routePattern` to include Unicode digits (`\p{N}`) and letters (`\p{L}`) if you’re using a modern JS engine (`/^\p{L}{0,2}\-?\p{N}{1,4}\p{L}?$/u`). |
| **Layer is a composite (e.g., separate icon‑only and text‑only layers)**. | You may need to **pair** layers by `source-layer` and `filter` to see if an icon‑only layer is paired with a numeric text‑only layer. That’s more advanced but doable. |

---

## Quick sanity check

If you run the function on a typical MapLibre style (e.g., the **MapLibre Streets** demo), you’ll see:

| Layer ID | `icon-image` | `text-field` | `isProbablyShield` |
|----------|--------------|--------------|--------------------|
| `road-number-shield` | `road-shield-1` | `{ref}` | **true** |
| `road-number-shield-outline` | `road-shield-outline-1` | `{ref}` | **true** |
| `place-label-city` | *none* | `{name_en}` | **false** |
| `poi-label` | `poi-circle` | `{name}` | **false** |

---

## Bottom line

*The heuristic based on `text-anchor` and `symbol-placement` is **too generic** and will mis‑classify most point‑placement symbol layers.*  
Instead, **key off the presence of a shield‑type `icon-image` (or a sprite name that matches a shield pattern), the use of `icon-text-fit`, and a numeric/route‑like `text-field`**. Adding a simple regex for route numbers and checking overlap flags will dramatically reduce false positives while catching the real shield layers.

Feel free to adapt the sample function to the specifics of the style you’re auditing (e.g., add your own sprite‑name whitelist or tweak the route‑number regex). Happy auditing!
